                <td>{int(genre_stats.loc['Fiction', 'Reviews']):,}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Non-Fiction</td>
                <td>${genre_stats.loc['Non Fiction', 'Price']:.2f}</td>
                <td>{genre_stats.loc['Non Fiction', 'User Rating']:.2f}</td>
                <td>{int(genre_stats.loc['Non Fiction', 'Reviews']):,}</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong> Fiction bestsellers are generally priced lower but generate substantially more reviews (likely due to broader mass-market appeal). Non-Fiction commands a premium price (${genre_stats.loc['Non Fiction', 'Price']:.2f} vs ${genre_stats.loc['Fiction', 'Price']:.2f}) while maintaining an identical average customer satisfaction level. Readers are willing to pay more for Non-Fiction (often instructional, biographical, or historical) without penalizing it in ratings.</p>
        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{genre_b64}" alt="Genre Comparison Bar Charts">
        </div>

        <h2 id="temporal-trends">4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
        <p>Tracking these metrics over the 2009–2019 window highlights major shifts in market dynamics.</p>
        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{trend_b64}" alt="Year-over-Year Trends">
        </div>
        <p><strong>Inflection Points:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Pricing Drop (2013-2015):</strong> There is a visible downward trend in the average price of bestsellers, particularly in Fiction, bottoming out around 2014-2015. This correlates with the rise of mass-market Kindle/e-book adoption, where sub-$10 pricing became highly prevalent.</li>
            <li><strong>Rating Inflation (2012-2019):</strong> User ratings trend upward over the decade, especially in Fiction, converging near 4.7-4.8 in later years. This suggests an overall improvement in perceived book quality, or a cultural shift in how Amazon users award 5-star reviews.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="top-authors">5. Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</h2>
        <p>Certain authors have established recurring dominance on the bestseller list. Here are the top 10 authors by frequency of appearance:</p>
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Author</th>
                <th>Bestseller Appearances</th>
                <th>Avg Price</th>
                <th>Avg Rating</th>
            </tr>
            {top_authors_rows}
        </table>
        <p>Authors like Jeff Kinney and Rick Riordan dominate through high-volume, serialized fiction at accessible price points while maintaining near-perfect ratings. Conversely, authors like Gary Chapman or the American Psychological Association maintain bestseller status for reference/non-fiction works across multiple years.</p>

        <h2 id="recommendations" class="recommendations">6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
        <h3>Actionable Insights for Publishers and Retailers</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Do Not Fear Pricing Non-Fiction at a Premium:</strong> The data proves Non-Fiction safely absorbs higher price points (${genre_stats.loc['Non Fiction', 'Price']:.2f} avg) without suffering rating penalties. Price instructional and reference books based on perceived value, not aggressive discounting.</li>
            <li><strong>Adopt Aggressive Pricing for Fiction Market Share:</strong> Fiction readers are more price-sensitive (average ${genre_stats.loc['Fiction', 'Price']:.2f}) but drive vastly higher review volumes (over {int(genre_stats.loc['Fiction', 'Reviews']):,} avg reviews). Lower pricing in Fiction is an effective loss-leader strategy to build massive, highly engaged audiences.</li>
            <li><strong>Focus on Serialized or Repeatable Formats:</strong> The dominance of authors like Jeff Kinney and Rick Riordan shows that serialized releases build entrenched bestseller consistency. Developing series allows publishers to lock in high ratings and recurring revenue.</li>
            <li><strong>Quality Expectations Have Risen:</strong> With average bestseller ratings now floating above 4.6 in recent years, a "good" rating of 4.3 or 4.4 is no longer competitive for the top 50 list. Editorial and pre-release vetting must aim for near-perfect customer satisfaction to achieve bestseller status in today's market.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</body>
